\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {3.3}Additive noise reshapes the phenotypic landscape toward high-expression states, modulated by reachability and threshold margin}{12}{subsection.3.3}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {Effect of Additive noise on the phenotypic landscape of cell-fate decision networks} \textbf  {(A)} Occupancy of $(1, 1)$ and $(1, 0)$ states vs.\ noise level for the TS network, a random sample of 100 parameter-set trajectories (gray) with the mean across the full parameter population (dashed). \textbf  {(B)} Occupancy boxplots for the TS network, monostable vs.\ bistable parameter sets, all-high vs.\ single-high states, with paired Wilcoxon significance brackets; ``*'' $p-value<0.05$, ``ns'' not significant. \textbf  {(C)} Reachability scatter for TS monostable parameter sets at $\sigma = 0.1$: each node's (max expression $-$ threshold) margin on the x/y axes, colored by occupancy of the (1,1) state. \textbf  {(D)} Spearman correlation between the minimum reachability margin across nodes and occupancy of the (1,1) state, across four noise levels (0.001, 0.01, 0.1, 1), TS monostable parameter sets. \textbf  {(E)} Occupancy vs.\ noise level for TT and TTSA, single-high $\to $ double-high $\to $ all-high state classes shown as Mean $\pm $ SD across all parameters, with paired Wilcoxon significance brackets between adjacent classes; ``*'' $p-value<0.05$, ``ns'' not significant. \textbf  {(F)} Heatmap of the Spearman correlation between minimum reachability margin and occupancy of the all-high state, across networks (TS, TSSA, TT, TTSA), noise levels, and stability class (monostable vs.\ multistable); ``*'' $p-value<0.05$, ``ns'' not significant. \textbf  {(G)} Joint time-averaged expression of nodes A and B per trajectory, as a fraction of each parameter set's maximum ($g/k$), for TS and TT without noise (C0) and under additive noise ($\sigma = 0.1$, $\Delta t = 0.01$; 2,000 TS and 2,568 TT parameter sets $\times $ 100 trajectories). For TT, node C is not shown. Colours rank trajectories by local density: the darkest region is the smallest area holding 50\% of trajectories, and the next two bands add 25\% and 15\% (highest-density regions of a two-dimensional kernel density estimate). Dashed lines: the ensemble discretization thresholds of A and B in units of $g/k$. Each threshold is a single absolute value, so in these units it differs between parameter sets; the line is the median over parameter sets and the grey band the interquartile range. Numbers give the fraction of trajectories in each quadrant, using each parameter set's own threshold, so trajectories above the median line can still be classified low. For TT, the all-high fraction (A, B and C high) is also given. All networks and $\sigma $ values are shown in Figure~\ref {fig:3S_expr}. }}{13}{figure.caption.3}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:3}{{3}{13}{\textbf {Effect of Additive noise on the phenotypic landscape of cell-fate decision networks} \textbf {(A)} Occupancy of $(1, 1)$ and $(1, 0)$ states vs.\ noise level for the TS network, a random sample of 100 parameter-set trajectories (gray) with the mean across the full parameter population (dashed). \textbf {(B)} Occupancy boxplots for the TS network, monostable vs.\ bistable parameter sets, all-high vs.\ single-high states, with paired Wilcoxon significance brackets; ``*'' $p-value<0.05$, ``ns'' not significant. \textbf {(C)} Reachability scatter for TS monostable parameter sets at $\sigma = 0.1$: each node's (max expression $-$ threshold) margin on the x/y axes, colored by occupancy of the (1,1) state. \textbf {(D)} Spearman correlation between the minimum reachability margin across nodes and occupancy of the (1,1) state, across four noise levels (0.001, 0.01, 0.1, 1), TS monostable parameter sets. \textbf {(E)} Occupancy vs.\ noise level for TT and TTSA, single-high $\to $ double-high $\to $ all-high state classes shown as Mean $\pm $ SD across all parameters, with paired Wilcoxon significance brackets between adjacent classes; ``*'' $p-value<0.05$, ``ns'' not significant. \textbf {(F)} Heatmap of the Spearman correlation between minimum reachability margin and occupancy of the all-high state, across networks (TS, TSSA, TT, TTSA), noise levels, and stability class (monostable vs.\ multistable); ``*'' $p-value<0.05$, ``ns'' not significant. \textbf {(G)} Joint time-averaged expression of nodes A and B per trajectory, as a fraction of each parameter set's maximum ($g/k$), for TS and TT without noise (C0) and under additive noise ($\sigma = 0.1$, $\Delta t = 0.01$; 2,000 TS and 2,568 TT parameter sets $\times $ 100 trajectories). For TT, node C is not shown. Colours rank trajectories by local density: the darkest region is the smallest area holding 50\% of trajectories, and the next two bands add 25\% and 15\% (highest-density regions of a two-dimensional kernel density estimate). Dashed lines: the ensemble discretization thresholds of A and B in units of $g/k$. Each threshold is a single absolute value, so in these units it differs between parameter sets; the line is the median over parameter sets and the grey band the interquartile range. Numbers give the fraction of trajectories in each quadrant, using each parameter set's own threshold, so trajectories above the median line can still be classified low. For TT, the all-high fraction (A, B and C high) is also given. All networks and $\sigma $ values are shown in Figure~\ref {fig:3S_expr}}{figure.caption.3}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {3.4}Increased occupancy of all-high states is caused by both the $\lambda $ distribution and stochasticity}{15}{subsection.3.4}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {The additive noise effect combines a shift in the $\lambda $ distribution with a contribution from time variation of $\lambda $.} Additive noise, $dt = 0.01$ unless stated. \textbf  {(A)} Distribution of the regulatory fold-change $\lambda $ visited during the stochastic simulations, for (i) inhibitory and (ii) activating edges, at four noise levels. \textbf  {(B)} TS: per-parameter-set resampling effect (frozen-$\lambda $ occupancy, C4, minus original RACIPE occupancy, C0) and stochastic effect (stochastic occupancy, C1, minus C4) of the all-high state, at every noise level. Diamonds, means; violins, distribution over 2{,}000 parameter sets. C4 uses 1{,}000 frozen draws per parameter set from that set's own stochastic trajectories. \textbf  {(C)} TS, $\sigma = 0.1$: frozen-$\lambda $ versus stochastic occupancy of the all-high state; each point is a parameter set and the line is the diagonal. \textbf  {(D)} TS: Occupancy of the all-high state (mean $\pm $ SD over parameter sets) across the sequence of controls, for monostable and multistable parameter sets (rows) at $\sigma = 0.01$, 0.1 and 1 (columns). C0, original RACIPE parameters; C1, stochastic simulation; C2, stochastic simulation with update interval $dt = 1$ and 10; C3, $\lambda $ drawn independently from the visited distribution and held for $T_\text  {hold} = 0.01$--100 (memoryless); C4, frozen $\lambda $; C5, every edge fixed at its mean $\lambda $. Colour, time for which a value of $\lambda $ persists; filled symbols, noise process with memory; open symbols, memoryless resampling. Paired Wilcoxon tests between adjacent conditions and between C2 and C3 at equal hold time, Benjamini--Hochberg corrected; only non-significant pairs (ns, adjusted $p \geq 0.05$) are marked. \textbf  {(E)} Decomposition for all seven networks (mean $\pm $ SD over parameter sets of the per-set difference in all-high occupancy): change in mean regulatory strength (C5 $-$ C0), effect of fast fluctuations beyond the mean (C3 at $T_\text  {hold} = 0.01$ $-$ C5), effect of time variation (C1 $-$ C4), and effect of memory at a hold time of 1 (C2 at $dt = 1$ $-$ C3 at $T_\text  {hold} = 1$; at $\sigma = 0.01$ this also includes the narrower $\lambda $ distribution reached at long $dt$). ns, one-sample Wilcoxon test, Benjamini--Hochberg adjusted $p \geq 0.05$. Production run, up to 1{,}000 parameter sets per stability class.}}{16}{figure.caption.4}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:3b}{{4}{16}{\textbf {The additive noise effect combines a shift in the $\lambda $ distribution with a contribution from time variation of $\lambda $.} Additive noise, $dt = 0.01$ unless stated. \textbf {(A)} Distribution of the regulatory fold-change $\lambda $ visited during the stochastic simulations, for (i) inhibitory and (ii) activating edges, at four noise levels. \textbf {(B)} TS: per-parameter-set resampling effect (frozen-$\lambda $ occupancy, C4, minus original RACIPE occupancy, C0) and stochastic effect (stochastic occupancy, C1, minus C4) of the all-high state, at every noise level. Diamonds, means; violins, distribution over 2{,}000 parameter sets. C4 uses 1{,}000 frozen draws per parameter set from that set's own stochastic trajectories. \textbf {(C)} TS, $\sigma = 0.1$: frozen-$\lambda $ versus stochastic occupancy of the all-high state; each point is a parameter set and the line is the diagonal. \textbf {(D)} TS: Occupancy of the all-high state (mean $\pm $ SD over parameter sets) across the sequence of controls, for monostable and multistable parameter sets (rows) at $\sigma = 0.01$, 0.1 and 1 (columns). C0, original RACIPE parameters; C1, stochastic simulation; C2, stochastic simulation with update interval $dt = 1$ and 10; C3, $\lambda $ drawn independently from the visited distribution and held for $T_\text {hold} = 0.01$--100 (memoryless); C4, frozen $\lambda $; C5, every edge fixed at its mean $\lambda $. Colour, time for which a value of $\lambda $ persists; filled symbols, noise process with memory; open symbols, memoryless resampling. Paired Wilcoxon tests between adjacent conditions and between C2 and C3 at equal hold time, Benjamini--Hochberg corrected; only non-significant pairs (ns, adjusted $p \geq 0.05$) are marked. \textbf {(E)} Decomposition for all seven networks (mean $\pm $ SD over parameter sets of the per-set difference in all-high occupancy): change in mean regulatory strength (C5 $-$ C0), effect of fast fluctuations beyond the mean (C3 at $T_\text {hold} = 0.01$ $-$ C5), effect of time variation (C1 $-$ C4), and effect of memory at a hold time of 1 (C2 at $dt = 1$ $-$ C3 at $T_\text {hold} = 1$; at $\sigma = 0.01$ this also includes the narrower $\lambda $ distribution reached at long $dt$). ns, one-sample Wilcoxon test, Benjamini--Hochberg adjusted $p \geq 0.05$. Production run, up to 1{,}000 parameter sets per stability class}{figure.caption.4}{}}
