\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {3.6}Multiplicative noise drives the phenotypic landscape toward single-high states - an opposite effect to that of additive noise}{18}{subsection.3.6}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5}{\ignorespaces Effect of multiplicative noise on the phenotypic landscape of cell-fate decision networks. \textbf  {(A)} Occupancy vs.\ noise level for the TTSA network, single-high and double-high states; grey lines show a random sample of 100 parameter-set trajectories, with the mean across the full parameter population shown separately. \textbf  {(B)} Average occupancy of single-high vs.\ double-high states vs.\ noise level, for TS, TSSA, TT, and TTSA. \textbf  {(C)} Same, for the 4-node networks TT4 and TS4. \textbf  {(D)} Decomposition of the change in single-high occupancy for all seven networks (mean $\pm $ SD over parameter sets of the per-set difference, as in Figure~\ref {fig:3b}E): shift in the $\lambda $ distribution (frozen $\lambda $, C4, $-$ C0), change in mean regulatory strength alone (mean $\lambda $, C5, $-$ C0), and time variation of $\lambda $ (stochastic, C1, $-$ C4), at $\sigma = 0.01$, 0.1 and 1. ns, one-sample Wilcoxon test, Benjamini--Hochberg adjusted $p \geq 0.05$; all other differences have adjusted $p < 0.05$. All panels: multiplicative (geometric) rule with maximal regulation $10^{3}$-fold ($\mu _{\min } = 10^{-3}$), $dt = 0.01$; for TS and TSSA the all-high state is counted as double-high. }}{19}{figure.caption.5}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:4}{{5}{19}{Effect of multiplicative noise on the phenotypic landscape of cell-fate decision networks. \textbf {(A)} Occupancy vs.\ noise level for the TTSA network, single-high and double-high states; grey lines show a random sample of 100 parameter-set trajectories, with the mean across the full parameter population shown separately. \textbf {(B)} Average occupancy of single-high vs.\ double-high states vs.\ noise level, for TS, TSSA, TT, and TTSA. \textbf {(C)} Same, for the 4-node networks TT4 and TS4. \textbf {(D)} Decomposition of the change in single-high occupancy for all seven networks (mean $\pm $ SD over parameter sets of the per-set difference, as in Figure~\ref {fig:3b}E): shift in the $\lambda $ distribution (frozen $\lambda $, C4, $-$ C0), change in mean regulatory strength alone (mean $\lambda $, C5, $-$ C0), and time variation of $\lambda $ (stochastic, C1, $-$ C4), at $\sigma = 0.01$, 0.1 and 1. ns, one-sample Wilcoxon test, Benjamini--Hochberg adjusted $p \geq 0.05$; all other differences have adjusted $p < 0.05$. All panels: multiplicative (geometric) rule with maximal regulation $10^{3}$-fold ($\mu _{\min } = 10^{-3}$), $dt = 0.01$; for TS and TSSA the all-high state is counted as double-high}{figure.caption.5}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {paragraph}{Origin of the multiplicative effect.}{20}{figure.caption.6}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {paragraph}{Dependence on the maximal regulatory strength.}{20}{figure.caption.6}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{sec:mult_floor}{{3.6}{20}{Dependence on the maximal regulatory strength}{figure.caption.6}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {Fate resolution of double-high states in TT4 under multiplicative noise.} \textbf  {(A)} Switching rate (switching events between discrete states per timestep, after the burn-in window) by noise level, additive versus multiplicative noise, for parameter sets grouped by their number of noise-free steady states; diamonds mark the mean. \textbf  {(B)} Fraction of the parameter sets with only double-high noise-free steady states in each stability class: monostable, bistable with mirror states (no shared high node), and bistable with non-mirror states (one shared high node). \textbf  {(C)} For each class in B, fraction of parameter sets accessing 1, 2, 3 or 4 distinct single-high states under multiplicative noise. A state counts as accessed if its occupancy is at least 10\% of that of the most occupied single-high state; for monostable sets the two single-high states not adjacent to the original state are pooled. \textbf  {(D)} Chord diagram of occupancy from each monostable double-high state (bottom) to the single-high states (top), $\sigma = 0.01$. \textbf  {(E)} Noise level $\to $ outcome alluvial for the monostable double-high sets, with outcomes relative to the initial double-high state XY (XY to X and XY to Y, adjacent single-high states; XY to Z/W, non-adjacent single-high states; XY to XY, original state; XY to other, any other state), colored by the classification in C. Ribbon widths are normalized within each noise level. Multiplicative noise follows the geometric rule ($\mu _{\min } = 10^{-3}$), $dt = 0.01$. }}{21}{figure.caption.6}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:6}{{6}{21}{\textbf {Fate resolution of double-high states in TT4 under multiplicative noise.} \textbf {(A)} Switching rate (switching events between discrete states per timestep, after the burn-in window) by noise level, additive versus multiplicative noise, for parameter sets grouped by their number of noise-free steady states; diamonds mark the mean. \textbf {(B)} Fraction of the parameter sets with only double-high noise-free steady states in each stability class: monostable, bistable with mirror states (no shared high node), and bistable with non-mirror states (one shared high node). \textbf {(C)} For each class in B, fraction of parameter sets accessing 1, 2, 3 or 4 distinct single-high states under multiplicative noise. A state counts as accessed if its occupancy is at least 10\% of that of the most occupied single-high state; for monostable sets the two single-high states not adjacent to the original state are pooled. \textbf {(D)} Chord diagram of occupancy from each monostable double-high state (bottom) to the single-high states (top), $\sigma = 0.01$. \textbf {(E)} Noise level $\to $ outcome alluvial for the monostable double-high sets, with outcomes relative to the initial double-high state XY (XY to X and XY to Y, adjacent single-high states; XY to Z/W, non-adjacent single-high states; XY to XY, original state; XY to other, any other state), colored by the classification in C. Ribbon widths are normalized within each noise level. Multiplicative noise follows the geometric rule ($\mu _{\min } = 10^{-3}$), $dt = 0.01$}{figure.caption.6}{}}
